{"answer":"To select a community leader, we will hold a simple vote at the beginning of each fishing season. All fishers present will write their names on a piece of bark and the community will gather in the common hut. The leader is the person who receives a majority of the written votes. If no one gets a majority, the top two vote‑givers will hold a quick spoken debate and the community will then vote again between them. The elected leader will serve until the end of the season and will then step down so another fisher can be chosen. This rotation keeps leadership fair and prevents any single person from gaining too much influence.

The policy stays as you wrote it:
"Each fisher may take no more than 8 kg per trip, and the community will collectively reserve 10 % of the lake’s current biomass each day for a communal buffer, replenishing it in subsequent fishing seasons. Every trip, Toa and all community members record their catch on a shared ledger. At the end of each day, the community leader calculates the lake’s biomass and enforces the 10 % reserve by setting a minimum amount of fish to be left in the lake. If a fisher exceeds the 8 kg limit, they must donate the excess to the communal reserve and are fined 5 % of the overage, payable to a community fund used for repairing nets and maintaining fishing gear. The leader reviews the ledger weekly and publicly announces any infractions."","revised_policy":"Each fisher may take no more than 8 kg per trip, and the community will collectively reserve 10 % of the lake’s current biomass each day for a communal buffer, replenishing it in subsequent fishing seasons. Every trip, Toa and all community members record their catch on a shared ledger. At the end of each day, the community leader calculates the lake’s biomass and enforces the 10 % reserve by setting a minimum amount of fish to be left in the lake. If a fisher exceeds the 8 kg limit, they must donate the excess to the communal reserve and are fined 5 % of the overage, payable to a community fund used for repairing nets and maintaining fishing gear. The leader reviews the ledger weekly and publicly announces any infractions.","revised_operationalization":"Selection of the community leader is made by majority vote at the start of each fishing season, with a runoff between the top two if necessary. The elected leader then serves until the end of the season, after which a new election takes place.","reasoning":"I kept the policy unchanged because the selection process didn’t alter the rules for catching or reserves. I added a clear, fair method for choosing a leader that relies on consensus and rotation, which should maintain community trust and fairness. The operationalization simply states the voting procedure I described. The reasoning explains why I chose a rotating majority vote to avoid power concentration."}

{"answer":"The leader will perform a short, standardized sampling each dawn. A small crew (including the leader) will set a calibrated seine net of fixed length and mesh size in a predetermined transect that represents the lake’s diversity. The net will be run for a fixed time (e.g., 15 minutes) and all fish caught will be weighed. The total weight of that sample, divided by the net area, gives an average biomass density (kg/m²). Multiplying this density by the lake’s total surface area yields an estimate of the lake’s current biomass. The leader will then calculate 10 % of this estimated biomass and designate that amount as the communal buffer, ensuring it is left in the lake. This estimate, along with the daily catch ledger, will be published so all fishers know the remaining stock and the required reserve.

This method balances precision with practicality; it uses a repeatable sample, can be done quickly each day, and its results are transparent to the whole community.
